= Related work <related>

*Design and security.* LSH was presented at ICISC 2014 @kim2014lsh. Hao @hao2016cryptanalysis found free-start collisions and pseudo-preimages for the full compression function, and boomerang distinguishers on 14 steps. The free-start attacks are cheap because the compression function has no feed-forward. None of these attacks reaches the hash function itself.

*One message with SIMD.* The steps of LSH apply the same operation to eight column pairs, so the sixteen words of the state fit in four 128-bit or two 256-bit registers. Pack et al. @pack2016parallel used SSE and AVX this way. Kim et al. @kim2019simd conjugated each step by a word permutation. This removes shuffles and gained about 10% over KISA's SIMD code across SSE2, SSSE3, XOP and AVX2. Choi et al. @choi2024parallel and Lee et al. @lee2026avx512 moved the state into AVX-512. Lee et al. report 1.16× for LSH-256 and 1.61× for LSH-512 over KISA's AVX2 code, and 1.55 cycles per byte for LSH-512 on 4096-byte messages on an Intel Tiger Lake core. On ARM, Bang et al. @bang2026batched rebuilt these techniques, and those of Sim's thesis @sim2023thesis, in NEON assembly. One message ran at 0.75 to 0.90 times the speed of KISA's portable C compiled by clang, and at 1.08 times under GCC. They trace the loss to the serial add-rotate-XOR chain of each column pair, to NEON's lack of a rotate instruction, and to an LSH-512 message schedule that does not fit in the 32 vector registers. Becker and Kannwischer @becker2022hybrid split Keccak between the scalar and vector units of AArch64 cores, a design Bang et al. left out of scope.

*Many messages.* Gueron and Krasnov @gueron2012simultaneous hashed independent messages in the lanes of SSE and AVX registers to speed up SHA-1 and SHA-2. Choi et al. @choi2024parallel batched LSH across GPU threads. Lee et al. @lee2026avx512 put two LSH-256 messages in the two 256-bit halves of an AVX-512 register, for 2.23 to 2.31 times the throughput of two calls to KISA's AVX2 code. Bang et al. @bang2026batched hashed two and four LSH-256 messages across NEON lanes, for 1.53 to 1.66 and about 2.0 times the throughput of sequential C. Their four-message core signs K-SPHINCS#super[+] 2.5 to 2.6 times faster. Two LSH-512 messages per register ran at 0.99 times sequential C. They set aside the SHA-3 instruction XAR. XAR rotates the XOR of its operands while the mix function rotates sums, so they judged that XAR could serve LSH-512 only as a bare rotation. Choi and Seo @choi2021pbkdf2 sped up PBKDF2-HMAC-LSH by precomputing the HMAC key blocks and spreading PBKDF2 blocks across threads.

*Software.* KISA's code has portable C, SSE2, SSSE3, XOP, AVX2 and NEON variants @kisa2026lsh, and SUPERCOP measures all of them on the machines of eBACS @bernstein2026ebacs. Crypto++ has portable, SSE and AVX2 code @dai2026cryptopp. go-krypto pairs portable Go with SSE2, SSSE3, AVX2 and NEON assembly @ryuanerin2026gokrypto. The Rust library allcrypt @allcrypt2026 and the lsh256 crate @lsh256rs2026 have portable code only. Each hashes one message per call, and each NEON LSH-512 rotates with a shift and a shift-and-insert. Published LSH work measures new code against KISA's; none compares implementations across languages.
